# Line endings, and why this file has to exist.
#
# The compiler reads CRLF now (#242) — a Windows user's own file compiles,
# and a CRLF program emits byte-identical output to its LF twin. So this
# file is no longer what makes the language work on Windows.
#
# It is still worth having. Several tests compare emitted output byte for
# byte, and a few fixtures are read from disk; a checkout that differed by
# platform would make those pass on one machine and fail on another for a
# reason nobody would look for. `eol=lf` makes a checkout identical
# everywhere.

# Everything the compiler reads, and everything that is compared byte for
# byte in a test.
*.zd    text eol=lf
*.rs    text eol=lf
*.js    text eol=lf
*.mjs   text eol=lf
*.css   text eol=lf
*.html  text eol=lf
*.json  text eol=lf
*.toml  text eol=lf
*.md    text eol=lf
*.yml   text eol=lf
*.yaml  text eol=lf
*.py    text eol=lf

# Shell scripts must be LF whatever the platform: a CRLF shebang line
# makes the kernel look for an interpreter whose name ends in a carriage
# return, and the error it gives says the file does not exist.
*.sh    text eol=lf

# Not text, and must never be line-ending converted.
*.png   binary
*.jpg   binary
*.gif   binary
*.ico   binary
*.woff  binary
*.woff2 binary
